      "math_academy_id": "e-11007",
      "problem": "If $\\sec x =2 \\sqrt 2$ then what is $\\sec(x+8\\pi)?$",
      "worked_solution": "The period of $y = \\sec x$ is $2\\pi.$ Therefore, for integer $n$ we have\n\n$$\n\\sec{x} = \\sec{(x+2n\\pi)} .\n$$\n\nSubstituting $n=4$ into the above gives\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sec}⁡x & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+2\\cdot 4\\cdot \\pi ) \\\\  & =\\operatorname{sec}⁡(x+8\\pi ).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, if $\\sec{x} = 2\\sqrt 2,$ then $\\sec{(x+8\\pi)} = 2\\sqrt 2.$",
